 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 3 gene records for Li-Fraumeni syndrome: tumor protein p53 [1], checkpoint kinase 2 [2] and cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [3], linked to 1 disease: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [6]. \n\nLi-Fraumeni syndrome is associated with the tumor protein p53 gene NCBIGene:7157 [1], the checkpoint kinase 2 gene NCBIGene:11200 [2], and the cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A gene NCBIGene:1029 [3]. \n\nLi-Fraumeni syndrome is a cancer predisposition syndrome caused by a germline mutation of the TP53 gene on chromosome 17p13.1, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with high penetrance [4]. The encoded protein p53 carries out genome-preserving functions during cellular stress, and loss of these functions permits development of multiple malignancies throughout life [4]. \n\nClinical variability is common in Li-Fraumeni syndrome, with phenotypic heterogeneity observed both among families carrying the same TP53 pathogenic variant and among members of the same family [5]. Possible contributors to this clinical spectrum include the type of TP53 mutation, polymorphic variants in TP53 or TP53-related genes, copy number variations, and epigenetic deregulation of TP53 expression [5]. \n\nThe classic component tumors of Li-Fraumeni syndrome include soft tissue sarcomas, osteosarcoma, premenopausal breast cancer, brain tumors, and adrenal cortical carcinomas [4]. \n\nDisease records found\n\nDisease name: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [6]. \n\nGene-to-disease mapping\n\nGene NCBIGene:1029, name: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [3]. Gene NCBIGene:11200, name: checkpoint kinase 2 [2]. Gene NCBIGene:7157, name: tumor protein p53 [1]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [7]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [12]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [14]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome heterogeneity [16]. \n\nMedgen records found\n\nMedgen title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [8]. Medgen clinical_features: MedGen lists no clinical features for Li-Fraumeni syndrome [9]. \n\nLiterature entity records found\n\nLiterature entity name: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [10]. \n\nClinical trial records found\n\nClinical trial name: Clinical, Genetic, Behavioral, Laboratory and Epidemiologic Characterization of Individuals and Families at High Risk of Breast/Ovarian Cancer [11]. Clinical trial name: Role of p53 Gene in Metabolism Regulation in Patients With Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [13]. Clinical trial name: Characterization of Patients With Uncommon Presentations and/or Uncommon Diseases Associated With the Cardiovascular System [15]. Clinical trial name: Clinical and Genetic Studies of Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [17]. Clinical trial name: LIFSCREEN : Evaluation of Whole Body MRI for Early Detection of Cancers in Subjects With P53 Mutation (Li-Fraumeni Syndrome) [18]. "
